  "why": "The paper identifies \"fuzzy matching\" as a feature in the Natural Language Understanding (NLU) component of Voice Assistants (VAs) that serves as a safeguard to tolerate minor user errors (termed LAPSUS) and ensure correct intent interpretation. The proposed fuzzer, LipFuzzer, is explicitly designed to discover inputs that bypass this safeguard. The failure oracle is defined as a LAPSUS being \"incorrectly interpreted by the VA system,\" which is a successful bypass. The mutation strategy is not random but is guided by linguistic models to generate plausible but confusing voice commands that are likely to evade the protection offered by simple fuzzy matching. The paper empirically demonstrates successful bypasses, showing generated LAPSUS that are not corrected by the system and lead to unintended outcomes, such as failing to install the correct application, while also acknowledging other generated LAPSUS that are successfully mitigated by the \"system's default fuzzy matching.\"",
